  content: `
<h2>Why Most Flashcards Fail</h2>
<p>Open the bag of a typical student a week before exams and you will often find a thick bundle of flashcards held with a rubber band. Ask how they use them and the answer is usually the same: <em>I read them over and over.</em> That is the mistake. Reading cards is just rereading in smaller pieces, and rereading creates a comfortable feeling of familiarity without teaching your memory to <strong>retrieve</strong> the answer when the page is blank.</p>
<p>The power of flashcards is not the cards. It is two ideas that cognitive psychologists have studied for a long time: <strong>active recall</strong> (pulling an answer out of memory instead of looking at it) and <strong>spacing</strong> (coming back to a fact after a gap instead of cramming it in one sitting). A flashcard is simply a convenient machine for doing both. If you use it without those two ideas, you get a slower way of rereading.</p>
<p>In this guide you will learn how to write cards that are worth studying, how to run a simple paper box system that handles the spacing for you, and how to fix the most common mistakes. Everything here is free and needs only paper, a pen and a few minutes a day.</p>

[[callout key|The two rules|Rule 1: always try to answer from memory before you flip the card. Rule 2: do not study a card again and again in one sitting. Come back to it after a gap.]]

<h2>What Flashcards Are Good At (and What They Are Not)</h2>
<p>Flashcards are excellent for material with a clear question and a short answer. In the subjects Nigerian students study for JAMB, WAEC and NECO, that covers a lot of ground.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chemistry:</strong> symbols and valencies, the reactivity series, tests for gases, definitions such as isotope and mole.</li>
<li><strong>Biology:</strong> functions of organelles, parts of the heart, hormones and their glands, classification terms.</li>
<li><strong>Physics:</strong> formulae with the meaning of each letter, units and their symbols, laws stated in one line.</li>
<li><strong>English:</strong> vocabulary and its meaning, figures of speech with an example, Oral English stress patterns.</li>
<li><strong>Government, Economics, Literature:</strong> definitions, features, causes and effects, authors and their works.</li>
</ul>
<p>They are weak at things that need practice rather than memory. You cannot learn to solve simultaneous equations or write a summary by flipping cards. Use flashcards to hold the <em>facts</em> (the formula, the rule, the definition), and use past questions for the <em>skill</em> of applying them. The best students do both.</p>

<h2>How to Write a Good Flashcard</h2>
<p>Most of the damage is done at the writing stage. A card that is vague, overloaded or copied word for word from the textbook is hard to remember and easy to fake. Follow these five rules.</p>
[[steps One fact per card::If the answer has three parts, make three cards or a numbered list you can test one part at a time ; Question on the front::Write a real question or a clear prompt rather than just a heading, so you must produce something ; Short answer on the back::Keep it to a line or two. If it needs a paragraph, you do not yet understand it well enough to turn it into a card ; Use your own words::Rewriting forces understanding. Copying from the book copies the words without the thinking ; Add a clue or picture::A small sketch of the cell, a circuit symbol or a memory hook makes recall easier]]
<h3>Weak cards versus strong cards</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Weak card</th><th>Strong card</th></tr>
<tr><td>Front: Photosynthesis<br>Back: (a whole paragraph)</td><td>Front: What are the raw materials of photosynthesis?<br>Back: Carbon dioxide and water, with light energy absorbed by chlorophyll</td></tr>
<tr><td>Front: Ohm's law<br>Back: V = IR</td><td>Front: In V = IR, what do V, I and R stand for and what are their units?<br>Back: V is voltage in volts, I is current in amperes, R is resistance in ohms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Front: Noun<br>Back: A naming word</td><td>Front: Underline the abstract noun: Her honesty impressed the panel<br>Back: honesty (a quality you cannot touch)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Front: Mitochondria<br>Back: Powerhouse</td><td>Front: Which organelle releases energy during respiration and where in the cell is it found?<br>Back: Mitochondria, in the cytoplasm</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The strong cards share one feature: the front asks you to produce something. The weak cards can be answered by recognising a word, and recognition is not the same as recall.</p>

[[qa Turn this textbook sentence into two flashcards: "Hydrogen is a gas that burns with a pop sound in air and is produced when a reactive metal like zinc reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid."|Find the separate facts. There is a test (the pop sound) and a preparation (zinc with acid) ; Card 1 front: What is the test for hydrogen gas? Card 1 back: A lighted splint makes a pop sound ; Card 2 front: Which two substances can you use to prepare hydrogen in the laboratory? Card 2 back: Zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid ; Optional card 3: Write the word equation. Back: zinc plus hydrochloric acid gives zinc chloride plus hydrogen|Two or three small cards, each testing one fact]]

<h2>Active Recall: The Right Way to Flip a Card</h2>
<p>Here is the exact routine for every card, every time.</p>
<ol>
<li>Read the front.</li>
<li><strong>Stop.</strong> Say or write the answer from memory. If you are studying with a partner, say it aloud.</li>
<li>Only then flip the card and compare.</li>
<li>Be honest. If you were half right, it counts as wrong. A vague feeling that you knew it is not an answer.</li>
</ol>
<p>The struggle in step 2 is the part that builds memory. If it feels uncomfortable, that is a good sign. If you find yourself flipping at once because the answer is obvious, the card is too easy and should be moved out of your daily pile or rewritten to ask for more detail.</p>
<p>Also practise both directions where it makes sense. For vocabulary, go from word to meaning and from meaning to word. For chemistry, go from a symbol to a name and from the name to the symbol. The reverse direction catches fragile knowledge that the forward direction misses.</p>

<h2>Spacing: Run a Leitner Box on Paper</h2>
<p>Your memory fades after you learn something, and each time you retrieve the fact, it fades more slowly. That is why a fact you review today, again in three days and again in a week lasts much longer than the same fact repeated ten times in one evening. You can run this on paper with five envelopes or a shoebox with dividers. This is called a Leitner box, named after Sebastian Leitner, the German science writer who popularised it.</p>
<h3>The setup</h3>
<p>Make three sections and label them Box 1, Box 2 and Box 3.</p>
[[timeline Box 1::Every new card starts here and is reviewed every day ; Box 2::Cards you answered correctly from Box 1 are reviewed every 3 days ; Box 3::Cards you answered correctly from Box 2 are reviewed once a week ; Any mistake::A wrong answer sends the card straight back to Box 1]]
<p>Each session goes like this: pick up the cards that are due, answer each from memory, and move the card up a box if you were right or back to Box 1 if you were wrong. Cards in Box 3 that you still get right after several weeks have earned a long rest. Keep them, and review them again the week before the exam.</p>
<h3>A real example of the workload</h3>
<p>Suppose you add 10 new cards each day for six days and you answer everything correctly. By day six you will have 60 cards in total, but you will not review 60 cards every day. Box 1 holds the newest cards that need daily review, Box 2 the cards due every third day, and Box 3 the cards due weekly. A typical day involves only part of the pile, usually 20 to 30 cards, and takes about ten to fifteen minutes. This is the big advantage of spacing: the pile grows, but the daily work stays manageable.</p>
<p>If you use a flashcard app, it does this calculation for you. Many free apps use a spaced-repetition algorithm so that you only see the cards that are due. The principle is the same as the paper box. Choose whichever you will actually keep up.</p>

[[bars Cards you should review today in a healthy system|New cards=10 ; Box 1 cards=10 ; Box 2 cards=6 ; Box 3 cards=3|cards]]
<p>The numbers above are only an illustration of how the load spreads out, not a fixed target. Your own numbers will depend on how many cards you add and how many you get right.</p>

<h2>How to Build a Daily Flashcard Routine</h2>
<p>A system is only useful if it is small enough to survive busy days. Here is a routine for a student preparing for exams.</p>
[[steps Morning, 10 minutes::Review the cards that are due. Do this before opening your phone for anything else ; After class, 10 minutes::Write 5 to 10 new cards from what you learned today, while it is still fresh ; Evening, 5 minutes::Quick second pass on the cards you missed in the morning ; Weekend, 30 minutes::Shuffle a mixed pile across subjects and test yourself in random order]]
<p>The writing step is part of the learning. Making cards from your own notes forces you to decide what really matters in a topic. Do not outsource it completely by downloading someone else's deck. Use shared cards as a supplement, but make your own for the topics where you struggle.</p>
<h3>Shuffle and mix</h3>
<p>If you always see the cards in the same order, you start remembering the answer from the position of the card, not from the content. Shuffle the pile each session. Once a week, mix cards from different subjects. In the exam, questions are not grouped by topic, so your practice should not be either.</p>
<h3>Combine flashcards with past questions</h3>
<p>After studying a topic through cards, do ten past questions on it. Whenever a past question exposes a gap, write a new card. Over time your deck becomes a map of exactly the facts that exams ask for.</p>

<h2>Eight Mistakes That Make Flashcards Useless</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Reading instead of recalling.</strong> Flipping at once defeats the purpose.</li>
<li><strong>Making cards too long.</strong> A paragraph on the back is a note, not a card.</li>
<li><strong>Copying the textbook.</strong> If you did not process it, you will not remember it.</li>
<li><strong>Making too many cards at once.</strong> Fifty new cards in one night create a mountain of reviews you will abandon.</li>
<li><strong>Never reviewing missed cards.</strong> The cards you fail are exactly the ones that need more attention.</li>
<li><strong>Cramming one batch repeatedly.</strong> Ten repetitions in one evening are far less durable than three repetitions across a week.</li>
<li><strong>Studying only your favourite subject.</strong> Cards for the subject you enjoy feel good, but the one you avoid needs them most.</li>
<li><strong>Believing cards are enough.</strong> Flashcards store facts, but you still need to practise solving problems and writing answers.</li>
</ol>

[[compare Common habit|Better habit|Reading cards over and over ; Making a hundred cards in one night ; Always using the same order ; Flipping when the answer feels familiar|Answering aloud before flipping ; Ten new cards a day ; Shuffling every session ; Marking half-right answers as wrong]]

<h2>Flashcard Ideas for Different Subjects</h2>
<p>Here are quick patterns you can copy for the subjects you are studying.</p>
<h3>Formulae and calculations</h3>
<p>Put the situation on the front, not just the name. For example: <em>A car moves from rest with acceleration 2 m/s<sup>2</sup> for 5 s. Which formula gives its final speed?</em> On the back write v = u + at, then v = 0 + 2 x 5 = 10 m/s. Practising the choice of formula is what you will need in the exam.</p>
<h3>Vocabulary and Oral English</h3>
<p>Write the word on one side and, on the other, its meaning plus a short sentence of your own. For Oral English, write the word with its stress pattern hidden and ask yourself to say it aloud before flipping.</p>
<h3>Diagrams</h3>
<p>Draw a diagram with some labels blank on the front. Fill them in from memory, then check against the back. This works well for the heart, the digestive system, the reproductive system of a flower and circuit symbols.</p>
<h3>Cloze (fill-in-the-blank) cards</h3>
<p>Take a sentence and hide the key word: <em>The gland that produces insulin is the ______.</em> Answer: pancreas. Cloze cards are quick to write and good for definitions and processes. Only hide one key idea per card.</p>

[[qa A student has a Leitner box. Today the Box 1 cards are due and she gets 7 of 10 correct. What happens to the cards?|Cards answered correctly move up. Seven cards go to Box 2 and will next be reviewed in three days ; Cards answered wrongly go back to or stay in Box 1. Three cards will be reviewed again tomorrow ; She writes down why each of the three failed, for example a confusing question or a missing detail, and rewrites the card if needed|7 cards move to Box 2 and 3 remain in Box 1]]

<h2>Digital or Paper?</h2>
<p>There is no winner. Paper cards cost almost nothing, never need data, and the act of writing can help you remember. They are also easy to hand to a friend for a quiz. The downside is that you must manage the boxes yourself. Phone apps handle the scheduling, can carry thousands of cards and let you review while on a bus. The downside is that notifications and other apps are one tap away. If you use an app, put your phone on do not disturb, finish the session and then close it. Whichever you choose, the habit matters more than the tool.</p>

<h2>Building a Card Deck for a Whole Subject</h2>
<p>Many students make cards at random and end up with a pile that does not match the exam. A better way is to build a deck from the syllabus. Take your subject syllabus and turn each topic into a small group of cards. For Biology, the topic of the cell might give you six cards: what is a cell, three organelles and their jobs, the difference between plant and animal cells, and one diagram card. For Chemistry, the topic of the periodic table might give you cards on groups, periods, trends and common elements.</p>
<p>Tag each card with its topic, for example a small letter in the corner, so you can pull out only the cards for one topic before a test. Keep a separate section for cards you keep missing. This leech pile deserves extra attention: look at each card and ask why it fails. Is the question unclear? Are there two facts on one card? Are you confusing it with a similar fact? Fix the card, add a clue or split it into smaller pieces. A card that you fail repeatedly is a warning that the card is badly written or the idea has not been understood.</p>
<p>Finally, retire cards. When a card has sat in the last box for a long time and you answer it instantly, put it in a box labelled Mastered and check it only in the last week before the exam. This keeps your active pile small and focused.</p>

<h2>What to Do Today</h2>
<p>Do not wait for a perfect system. Start with ten cards from the topic you studied most recently and run your first box session tomorrow.</p>
[[checklist Make a three-section Leitner box from a shoebox or three envelopes ; Write 10 cards from today's lesson with a question on the front and a short answer on the back ; Answer each card aloud before flipping it ; Mark half-right answers as wrong and move those cards back to Box 1 ; Schedule a 10-minute review at the same time tomorrow]]
`
